insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:fest-night','system-design','fest-night','Fest Night','Your college fest ticket site opens at 12:00 and 5,000 students hit it at once. The page crawls, and one student gets charged twice. You built it, so you fix it.','College fest ticketing site, one server, one SQL database, 200,000 ticket rows.','foundations',array['system-design:client-server','system-design:database-indexes','system-design:atomic-updates','system-design:idempotency','system-design:caching','system-design:rate-limiting']::text[],10,'live','A fast, correct ticket site comes from fixing the saturated tier first, making sales atomic, making payments idempotent, caching only what can be stale, and rate limiting on the right key. Bottleneck first: index the hot query before scaling anything. Sell a seat with one atomic conditional update. Idempotency key per payment attempt. Short-TTL cache for the listing, never for the buy decision. Rate limit per user or device, generous per IP.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:fest-night:1','system-design:fest-night',1,'initial','design_choice','At 12:00:05 the site is crawling. The web server CPU is at 20%, but the database CPU is pinned at 100%. The main query is: SELECT * FROM tickets WHERE event_id = ? AND status = ''available'' LIMIT 1, and the tickets table has no index besides the primary key. What do you do first?','The site is live and slow. Web servers look fine, the database is the one sweating.','[{"id":"a","text":"Add three more web servers behind a load balancer."},{"id":"b","text":"Add a composite index on (event_id, status) so the query stops scanning the whole table."},{"id":"c","text":"Restart the database to clear its memory."},{"id":"d","text":"Show a ''try again later'' page to half the students."}]'::jsonb,'b','The web tier is idle at 20% CPU and the database is saturated, so more web servers just send more queries to the bottleneck. Without an index the query scans up to 200,000 rows for every request. A composite index on (event_id, status) lets the database jump straight to matching rows. A restart fixes nothing and drops the cache. Turning students away is a last resort, not a first move.','First find which tier is saturated, then fix that tier. Scaling the wrong tier adds cost and no capacity.',array['system-design:database-indexes','system-design:client-server']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","web","db"],"links":[["students","web"],["web","db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:fest-night:2','system-design:fest-night',2,'probe','design_choice','The site is faster, but two students both received a ticket for seat B14. Your code reads the seat, checks that it is available, then updates it to ''sold''. Why did both succeed, and what is the fix?','The index fixed speed. Now concurrency exposes a correctness bug.','[{"id":"a","text":"The database is buggy. Switch to a different database."},{"id":"b","text":"Both requests read ''available'' before either wrote ''sold''. Use one atomic conditional update (UPDATE ... SET status=''sold'' WHERE id=? AND status=''available'') and check that one row changed."},{"id":"c","text":"Add a sleep of 100ms between the read and the write."},{"id":"d","text":"Run the app on a single thread."}]'::jsonb,'b','This is a check-then-act race: two requests interleave between the read and the write. Making the check and the write one atomic statement means the database serializes them, and exactly one request sees ''rows affected = 1''. A sleep only shrinks the window. A single thread does not help once you have more than one server or process.','Any ''read, decide in app code, write'' sequence on shared data is a race. Push the decision into the database operation.',array['system-design:atomic-updates']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","web","db(indexed)"],"links":[["students","web"],["web","db(indexed)"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:fest-night:3','system-design:fest-night',3,'probe','design_choice','A student''s payment call timed out, so the app retried, and the student was charged twice for one ticket. How do you make retries safe?','Races on seats are fixed. Now the payment call has an unknown outcome on timeout.','[{"id":"a","text":"Never retry payment calls."},{"id":"b","text":"Disable the Pay button after the first click."},{"id":"c","text":"Generate an idempotency key per order attempt, send it with the payment request, and enforce uniqueness on it so a retry returns the original result."},{"id":"d","text":"Retry only after waiting 10 seconds."}]'::jsonb,'c','On a timeout you do not know whether the charge happened. An idempotency key lets the gateway and your own database recognise a repeat of the same request and return the first result instead of charging again. Disabling the button is a UI nicety that does not stop network retries or double submits from two tabs. Never retrying loses legitimate payments, and waiting does not make a retry safe.','Timeouts mean ''unknown'', not ''failed''. Safe retries need a stable identity for the operation.',array['system-design:idempotency']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","web","db","payment-gateway"],"links":[["students","web"],["web","db"],["web","payment-gateway"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:fest-night:4','system-design:fest-night',4,'probe','design_choice','Thousands of students keep refreshing the event listing page (event name, date, price, how many tickets are left). It is identical for everyone and changes rarely. What is the sensible use of a cache here?','Writes are safe now, but the listing page still hits the database for every refresh.','[{"id":"a","text":"Cache the listing page for about 15 to 30 seconds, but never trust the cache for the actual ''buy'' decision."},{"id":"b","text":"Cache the buy endpoint''s result for 5 minutes."},{"id":"c","text":"Cache everything forever, including ticket counts."},{"id":"d","text":"Do not cache anything, because data might be stale."}]'::jsonb,'a','Reads that are identical for everyone and tolerate slight staleness are ideal for a short-TTL cache, which removes most database load. The decision to sell a ticket must still go through the atomic update, because a cached ''available'' could be wrong by the time someone buys. Caching the buy result or caching forever would sell tickets that are gone.','Cache reads that can be a little stale. Never cache the decision that must be exactly right.',array['system-design:caching']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","web","cache","db","payment-gateway"],"links":[["students","web"],["web","cache"],["cache","db"],["web","payment-gateway"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:fest-night:5','system-design:fest-night',5,'probe','design_choice','A script is hitting your buy endpoint hundreds of times a second from a few accounts to grab tickets for resale. Many real students share the same campus Wi-Fi, so they share one public IP. What do you do?','The site is fast and correct. Now someone is abusing it.','[{"id":"a","text":"Block the campus IP address."},{"id":"b","text":"Rate limit per logged-in user (and per device), with a small per-IP ceiling set high enough for a shared campus network."},{"id":"c","text":"Hide the buy button for the first minute."},{"id":"d","text":"Ask students to stop using scripts."}]'::jsonb,'b','Keying limits on the authenticated user or device targets the abuser. A per-IP limit alone would punish everyone behind the same campus NAT, so any IP ceiling must be generous. Blocking the whole IP locks out real students. Hiding the button or asking nicely does nothing against a script that calls the API directly.','Choose the rate-limit key carefully. Shared IPs make ''per IP'' a blunt instrument.',array['system-design:rate-limiting']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","bots","rate-limiter","web","cache","db"],"links":[["students","rate-limiter"],["bots","rate-limiter"],["rate-limiter","web"],["web","cache"],["cache","db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:follow-the-sun','system-design','follow-the-sun','Follow the Sun','Your fintech, Meridian, just got licences for India, Europe and the US in a single week. The board wants users everywhere to get fast responses and never lose a transaction.','Fintech with a single-region primary database expanding to three regions.','expert',array['system-design:multi-region-design','system-design:home-region-partitioning','system-design:replication-lag','system-design:read-your-writes','system-design:cross-region-transactions','system-design:failover-and-fencing','system-design:data-residency']::text[],16,'draft','Give each account one home region for writes, handle replication lag with read-your-writes, use idempotent ledger sagas for cross-region transfers, fence old leaders on failover, and shape replication around data residency. Home-region ownership avoids write conflicts. Read-your-writes via home-region reads or version tokens. Idempotent ledger steps and compensation, not cross-ocean locks. Consensus election with epoch fencing, explicit RPO. Residency-aware partitioning and replication.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:follow-the-sun:1','system-design:follow-the-sun',1,'initial','design_choice','Today everything runs in one region with a single primary database. Users on other continents see 200 to 300 ms latency on every request. You want low latency everywhere without conflicting writes on the same account. What is the cleanest multi-region data design?','Multi-region is easy for reads and hard for writes.','[{"id":"a","text":"Allow any region to write any account at any time, and sort out conflicts afterwards."},{"id":"b","text":"Assign each account a home region that owns its writes, serve it from there, and replicate to other regions for reads and recovery."},{"id":"c","text":"Keep one region and buy a faster network."},{"id":"d","text":"Copy the whole database to every region and let each edit freely."}]'::jsonb,'b','A designated writer per account avoids conflicting writes. Local reads can be fast where replicas are allowed, but an account whose home region is far away still has cross-region write latency. Geography, failover and consistency requirements must be measured and designed explicitly.','Avoid conflicts by design: one owner per piece of data beats clever merging.',array['system-design:home-region-partitioning','system-design:multi-region-design']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["India users","EU users","US users","single-region-primary"],"links":[["India users","single-region-primary"],["EU users","single-region-primary"],["US users","single-region-primary"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:follow-the-sun:2','system-design:follow-the-sun',2,'probe','design_choice','A user in India pays a bill, then travels to Europe and opens the app. The request hits the EU region, which has a replica that is 2 seconds behind. They see the old balance and panic. How do you keep their own writes visible to them?','Replication is asynchronous, so replicas lag.','[{"id":"a","text":"Tell users to wait a few seconds."},{"id":"b","text":"Provide read-your-writes: for balances and recent transactions, read from the home region, or carry a version or timestamp token from the write and only serve reads from a replica that has caught up to it."},{"id":"c","text":"Disable replicas."},{"id":"d","text":"Always show the user a cached balance."}]'::jsonb,'b','Asynchronous replication means a replica can serve stale data, which is unacceptable for a user''s own balance. Reading from the home region or passing a ''read at least this version'' token ensures users see their latest writes. Less critical reads can still use lagging replicas.','Choose consistency per read: critical reads go to the owner or wait for catch-up.',array['system-design:replication-lag','system-design:read-your-writes']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["user","EU region","EU replica (lagging)","India home region"],"links":[["user","EU region"],["EU region","EU replica (lagging)"],["India home region","EU replica (lagging)"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:follow-the-sun:3','system-design:follow-the-sun',3,'probe','tradeoff','A user in India sends money to a user in Europe. Their accounts are owned by different regions. Using a distributed two-phase commit across continents would block and fail under network problems. What is a more practical design?','A transaction now spans two home regions.','[{"id":"a","text":"Hold locks in both regions until both agree, however long it takes."},{"id":"b","text":"Use a ledger with idempotent steps: debit the sender in their region (moving money into an in-flight state), reliably send a transfer event with a unique id, credit the receiver idempotently, and compensate the debit if the credit cannot complete."},{"id":"c","text":"Credit first and debit later if there is time."},{"id":"d","text":"Disallow cross-region transfers."}]'::jsonb,'b','Cross-region commits that hold locks over long, unreliable links are slow and fragile. A saga-style ledger with durable events, unique transfer ids and idempotent credit and debit steps stays correct under retries and partitions, and compensation covers failures. Crediting first risks creating money from nothing.','Prefer idempotent steps with compensation to long distributed locks.',array['system-design:cross-region-transactions']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["India ledger","event stream","EU ledger","transfer-coordinator"],"links":[["transfer-coordinator","India ledger"],["transfer-coordinator","EU ledger"],["India ledger","event stream"],["event stream","EU ledger"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:follow-the-sun:4','system-design:follow-the-sun',4,'probe','design_choice','The US region''s primary fails. You promote a replica in another region, but the old primary was only partitioned, not dead, and comes back still believing it is the primary. Now two primaries accept writes. How do you prevent this split brain?','Failover creates a dangerous overlap if the old leader is merely unreachable.','[{"id":"a","text":"Hope the old primary stays down."},{"id":"b","text":"Use a consensus-backed leader election with an epoch or fencing token, so storage and replicas reject writes from any leader with an older epoch, and decide in advance how much replication lag (RPO) you will accept."},{"id":"c","text":"Let both primaries accept writes and merge later."},{"id":"d","text":"Reboot every server in the company."}]'::jsonb,'b','Fencing tokens make failover safe: once a new leader holds a higher epoch, any write from the stale leader is refused. You also need to be explicit that asynchronous replication means a failover can lose the last few seconds of writes (the recovery point objective), or use synchronous replication where that loss is unacceptable.','Failover correctness is about fencing the old leader, not just promoting a new one.',array['system-design:failover-and-fencing']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["old-primary","new-primary","epoch-service (consensus)","clients"],"links":[["old-primary","epoch-service (consensus)"],["new-primary","epoch-service (consensus)"],["clients","new-primary"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:follow-the-sun:5','system-design:follow-the-sun',5,'probe','design_choice','A regulator says EU customers'' personal and financial data must stay inside the EU. Your plan replicates every account to every region for resilience. What do you change?','Resilience and residency pull in opposite directions.','[{"id":"a","text":"Ignore it, since users will not know."},{"id":"b","text":"Partition by residency: keep EU customers'' personal data only in EU regions (using EU-only replicas for resilience), route requests by home region, and share only non-personal or anonymised data globally."},{"id":"c","text":"Move all data to the US."},{"id":"d","text":"Delete the EU users."}]'::jsonb,'b','Residency rules are a hard constraint, so replication topology must follow them: EU data replicates only to EU locations, and routing sends each user to their home region. Global features can run on non-personal or anonymised data. Disaster recovery stays possible within the permitted geography.','Legal constraints shape topology. Model them as part of the architecture, not afterwards.',array['system-design:data-residency']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["EU region (primary + EU-only DR)","India region","US region","global-router"],"links":[["global-router","EU region (primary + EU-only DR)"],["global-router","India region"],["global-router","US region"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:limited-stock-checkout','system-design','limited-stock-checkout','Who gets the last packets?','There are 340 packets of Maggi and 200,000 people adding them to cart. Where does the system make the stock promise?','Flash sale checkout with a single limited SKU','intermediate',array['system-design:inventory-reservation','system-design:atomic-stock']::text[],7,'live','Cart shows an estimate, not a promise. At Place order, atomically reserve one unit with a short expiry. The checkout path owns the critical counter; expired reservations return stock, and confirmed payment commits the order idempotently.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:limited-stock-checkout:1','system-design:limited-stock-checkout',1,'initial','design_choice','What does Add to cart mean for stock?',null,'[{"id":"a","text":"A best-effort availability hint, without reservation"},{"id":"b","text":"A permanent reservation until the browser closes"},{"id":"c","text":"An immediate purchase"}]'::jsonb,'a','A avoids trapping 340 units in abandoned carts. B can strand every unit. C charges or commits before checkout consent.','A cart is intent; it is not yet a stock guarantee.',array['system-design:inventory-reservation']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:limited-stock-checkout:2','system-design:limited-stock-checkout',2,'probe','design_choice','Two buyers click Place order for the final packet. How is one winner chosen?','Only one unreserved packet remains.','[{"id":"a","text":"One conditional atomic decrement or reservation transaction"},{"id":"b","text":"Read stock separately, then each writes stock minus one"},{"id":"c","text":"Have both browsers hide the button"}]'::jsonb,'a','A serializes the last-unit claim. B lets both see one and oversell. C is cosmetic and cannot arbitrate races across devices.','The invariant is reserved plus sold never exceeds 340.',array['system-design:atomic-stock']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:limited-stock-checkout:3','system-design:limited-stock-checkout',3,'probe','tradeoff','A buyer reserves one packet but abandons payment. What prevents stock staying trapped?','Payment has not completed after the hold deadline.','[{"id":"a","text":"A short reservation expiry and idempotent release"},{"id":"b","text":"Leave it reserved forever for fairness"},{"id":"c","text":"Let another buyer spend the same reservation ID"}]'::jsonb,'a','A returns the unit after the deadline. B permanently loses stock. C creates double-spend risk and breaks ownership.','Expiry recovery must work even if the browser never returns.',array['system-design:inventory-reservation']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:limited-stock-checkout:4','system-design:limited-stock-checkout',4,'probe','tradeoff','The cache says 12 left while the durable counter says 0. What should checkout do?','Cart reads a slightly stale availability cache.','[{"id":"a","text":"Reject reservation and explain that the estimate changed"},{"id":"b","text":"Accept the cached value as final"},{"id":"c","text":"Insert a negative stock row and reconcile later"}]'::jsonb,'a','A treats the authoritative counter as the promise. B oversells. C creates a debt the seller may be unable to fulfil.','Cached availability can guide display, never authorize the last unit.',array['system-design:atomic-stock']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:limited-stock-checkout:5','system-design:limited-stock-checkout',5,'probe','design_choice','How should the UI behave as stock approaches zero?','The cart page is receiving far more traffic than checkout.','[{"id":"a","text":"Show less certain availability and confirm only after reservation"},{"id":"b","text":"Promise stock as soon as the item appears in cart"},{"id":"c","text":"Run every cart view through the critical stock transaction"}]'::jsonb,'a','A is truthful and keeps the hot counter for checkout. B promises too early. C puts 200,000 cart views on a 340-unit contention path.','Only checkout needs to contend on the critical counter.',array['system-design:inventory-reservation','system-design:atomic-stock']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:maggi-flash-sale','system-design','maggi-flash-sale','Maggi Flash Sale','340 packets of Maggi are left and 2 lakh people have them in their carts. Reserve stock too early and inventory is trapped in abandoned carts. Reserve too late and checkout turns into a lottery. Where does the system make its promise?','Quick-commerce flash sale with a tiny inventory and enormous demand.','intermediate',array['system-design:inventory-reservation','system-design:cached-availability','system-design:atomic-decrement','system-design:ttl-reservations','system-design:hot-counter','system-design:honest-ux']::text[],12,'live','Make the promise at checkout. Keep browsing on cached availability, decide stock with an atomic decrement, hold it with an expiring reservation, and let the UI be honest as stock runs low. Cart reads cached availability with a safety buffer. Only checkout touches the authoritative counter. Atomic conditional decrement for the last unit. 5-minute reservation: RESERVED, then SOLD or back to AVAILABLE. Graduated UI messaging as stock gets low.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:maggi-flash-sale:1','system-design:maggi-flash-sale',1,'initial','design_choice','340 packets, 2 lakh people clicking Add to Cart. When should the system actually commit stock to a customer?','The core question of the whole campaign.','[{"id":"a","text":"On Add to Cart, so every cart is guaranteed."},{"id":"b","text":"At checkout, when the user commits to buying (Place order), not when they add to cart."},{"id":"c","text":"After payment completes, with no earlier claim."},{"id":"d","text":"Never, and just oversell and apologise."}]'::jsonb,'b','If every Add to Cart reserves stock, 340 clicks can exhaust inventory immediately, even though most carts will never become orders. Reserving only after payment makes people pay for goods they may not get. The balanced point is checkout commitment, with a short reservation while payment happens.','Reserve at the moment of commitment, not the moment of interest.',array['system-design:inventory-reservation']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["customers","cart-service","inventory-service"],"links":[["customers","cart-service"],["cart-service","inventory-service"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:maggi-flash-sale:2','system-design:maggi-flash-sale',2,'probe','design_choice','Millions of browsers are looking at the product page and cart, which show whether Maggi is available. How should these reads work?','Carts do not reserve stock, but they still need to show availability.','[{"id":"a","text":"Run a live inventory query against the authoritative store for each view."},{"id":"b","text":"Serve an approximate availability hint from a short-lived cache, but confirm the real stock only when the customer places the order."},{"id":"c","text":"Never show availability."},{"id":"d","text":"Always show ''in stock'' regardless."}]'::jsonb,'b','Browsing traffic should not hammer the authoritative stock counter. A cached hint can be stale even with a safety buffer, so the page must not promise a precise count or guarantee a packet. The checkout reservation decides whether stock is still available.','Keep high-volume browsing away from the stock write path, and label cached availability as an estimate.',array['system-design:cached-availability']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["customers","cart-service","availability-cache","inventory-db"],"links":[["customers","cart-service"],["cart-service","availability-cache"],["availability-cache","inventory-db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:maggi-flash-sale:3','system-design:maggi-flash-sale',3,'probe','design_choice','Two customers press Place order for the last packet within the same millisecond. How do you make sure exactly one gets it?','Checkout is where the scarce resource is actually decided.','[{"id":"a","text":"Read the stock, check it is above zero in application code, then write the new value."},{"id":"b","text":"Use one atomic operation that decrements only if the stock is at least 1 (for example, a conditional update or an atomic counter script), and treat ''not applied'' as sold out."},{"id":"c","text":"Let both succeed and sort it out later."},{"id":"d","text":"Use a random number to decide who wins."}]'::jsonb,'b','A conditional atomic decrement makes the check and the update one indivisible step, so two requests cannot both take the last unit. Reading and then writing in application code has a race window where both see stock 1.','Decide scarce stock with one atomic operation, not read-then-write. Only the small fraction of users who reach checkout compete for this one hot counter.',array['system-design:atomic-decrement','system-design:hot-counter']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["checkout-service","atomic-counter","inventory-db"],"links":[["checkout-service","atomic-counter"],["atomic-counter","inventory-db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:maggi-flash-sale:4','system-design:maggi-flash-sale',4,'probe','design_choice','A customer gets a packet reserved and then closes the app without paying. How does that packet come back for the next buyer?','Stock is now held during payment.','[{"id":"a","text":"Wait for the customer to cancel."},{"id":"b","text":"Give each reservation a short expiry (for example 5 minutes). On payment success it becomes SOLD, and on failure or timeout it returns to AVAILABLE."},{"id":"c","text":"Reserve forever to be safe."},{"id":"d","text":"Manually check abandoned reservations every day."}]'::jsonb,'b','A TTL guarantees that abandoned reservations free themselves without relying on user behaviour, and the explicit states (RESERVED to SOLD, or back to AVAILABLE) keep inventory accounting clean. Waiting for cancellation leaks stock whenever users vanish.','Temporary claims must expire on their own.',array['system-design:ttl-reservations']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["checkout-service","reservation-store(TTL)","payment","inventory"],"links":[["checkout-service","reservation-store(TTL)"],["checkout-service","payment"],["reservation-store(TTL)","inventory"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:maggi-flash-sale:5','system-design:maggi-flash-sale',5,'probe','tradeoff','Stock drops from 340 to 20. The page still shows a confident green ''In stock'' badge. What is the better product and system behaviour?','The cart is not a guarantee, so the UI should not pretend it is.','[{"id":"a","text":"Keep showing ''In stock'' until it hits 0."},{"id":"b","text":"Change the message as stock falls (''In stock'', then ''Only a few left'', then ''High demand, availability confirmed at checkout'') so the UI sets the right expectation."},{"id":"c","text":"Hide the product when stock is below 100."},{"id":"d","text":"Show the exact live count to the unit."}]'::jsonb,'b','Because carts do not hold inventory, the UI should communicate uncertainty as stock gets low. Graduated messaging manages expectations and reduces the shock of a failed checkout. Showing an exact count would invite constant refresh traffic and still be stale.','Correctness is also about honest expectations, not only backend guarantees.',array['system-design:honest-ux']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["customers","cart-ui","availability-cache"],"links":[["customers","cart-ui"],["cart-ui","availability-cache"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:midnight-notifications','system-design','midnight-notifications','12 AM Notification','Marketing wants to send a push notification to 10 million users at exactly 12:00 AM for the sale launch. Last time, the notification worked, and then your own servers went down.','Shopping app with 10M push-enabled users and a push provider with rate limits.','advanced',array['system-design:queues-and-workers','system-design:provider-rate-limits','system-design:priority-lanes','system-design:dedupe','system-design:load-shaping']::text[],14,'live','Batch and queue the audience, throttle to the provider''s limit with backoff, isolate critical traffic in priority lanes, dedupe by deterministic ids, and stagger the blast so you do not DDoS yourself. Batches on a queue, progress per batch. Shared rate limiter, backoff with jitter on 429. Separate transactional and promotional lanes. Deterministic notification ids and collapse keys. Stagger sends and pre-warm for the response spike.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:midnight-notifications:1','system-design:midnight-notifications',1,'initial','design_choice','A cron job loops over 10 million users and calls the push provider one by one from a single process. It will take hours and any crash loses progress. How do you restructure it?','A single loop is neither fast nor resumable.','[{"id":"a","text":"Split the audience into batches, put the batches on a queue, and let many workers process them in parallel, recording progress per batch."},{"id":"b","text":"Run the same loop on a faster machine."},{"id":"c","text":"Send email instead."},{"id":"d","text":"Start the loop at 9 PM and hope it finishes."}]'::jsonb,'a','Batches on a queue turn one fragile long job into many small resumable ones that scale with the number of workers. Recording progress per batch means a crash re-does only the unfinished batches, not all 10 million.','Parallelise by splitting the audience, and make each unit of work resumable.',array['system-design:queues-and-workers']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["campaign-service","cron-loop","push-provider"],"links":[["campaign-service","cron-loop"],["cron-loop","push-provider"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:midnight-notifications:2','system-design:midnight-notifications',2,'probe','design_choice','With 200 workers you start sending as fast as possible. The push provider starts returning rate-limit errors (HTTP 429) and your workers immediately retry in a loop, making it worse. What should the workers do?','The provider has its own capacity limits.','[{"id":"a","text":"Retry immediately until it works."},{"id":"b","text":"Throttle sending to the provider''s allowed rate with a shared limiter, back off on 429s with jitter, and let the queue absorb the backlog."},{"id":"c","text":"Drop all notifications that hit a 429."},{"id":"d","text":"Add more workers."}]'::jsonb,'b','The provider''s limit is a hard external constraint. A shared rate limiter keeps total throughput under it, backoff with jitter prevents retry storms, and the queue holds the rest. More workers would just hit the limit harder.','Your slowest dependency sets your real throughput. Respect it with backpressure.',array['system-design:provider-rate-limits']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["queue","workers","rate-limiter","push-provider"],"links":[["queue","workers"],["workers","rate-limiter"],["rate-limiter","push-provider"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:midnight-notifications:3','system-design:midnight-notifications',3,'probe','design_choice','During the blast, a user requests a login OTP push and a payment alert. Both are stuck behind millions of promotional messages. How do you fix that?','Not all notifications are equal.','[{"id":"a","text":"Use one queue and send everything first-come, first-served."},{"id":"b","text":"Use separate queues (priority lanes) for transactional and promotional traffic, with reserved capacity so critical messages never wait behind a blast."},{"id":"c","text":"Pause all OTPs during the sale."},{"id":"d","text":"Send promotions slower for everyone forever."}]'::jsonb,'b','Separating lanes and reserving capacity protects time-sensitive messages from bulk traffic. A single FIFO queue has head-of-line blocking, so a 10 million item promo blast delays a 1-item OTP by the whole backlog.','Isolate critical traffic from bulk traffic with separate queues and reserved capacity.',array['system-design:priority-lanes']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["transactional-queue","promo-queue","workers","push-provider"],"links":[["transactional-queue","workers"],["promo-queue","workers"],["workers","push-provider"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:midnight-notifications:4','system-design:midnight-notifications',4,'probe','design_choice','A worker crashes after sending a batch but before marking it done, so the batch is re-processed and users get the same promo twice. How do you avoid duplicates?','Queues redeliver. Users hate duplicates.','[{"id":"a","text":"Accept duplicates."},{"id":"b","text":"Use a stable (campaign, user) id with provider-side deduplication when supported; record send attempts and reconcile uncertain outcomes before retrying."},{"id":"c","text":"Never retry a batch."},{"id":"d","text":"Send everything twice to be safe."}]'::jsonb,'b','A crash after the provider accepts a send but before the ledger is marked leaves the outcome unknown. A local sent ledger alone cannot prevent a second external send. A provider idempotency or collapse key can suppress repeats when its contract supports that; otherwise reconcile the attempt and accept that delivery may be at least once.','A local ledger cannot make an external send exactly once across a crash boundary.',array['system-design:dedupe']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["queue","workers","sent-ledger","push-provider"],"links":[["queue","workers"],["workers","sent-ledger"],["workers","push-provider"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:midnight-notifications:5','system-design:midnight-notifications',5,'probe','tradeoff','Post-mortem: notifications went out fine, but within seconds millions of people opened the app together and your own API fell over. What is the better launch design?','The push worked. The synchronized response broke your backend.','[{"id":"a","text":"Send all 10 million at exactly 12:00:00 for maximum excitement."},{"id":"b","text":"Stagger the sends over a window (for example 10 to 15 minutes), cache and pre-warm the landing content and sale pages, and load test for the expected click-through burst."},{"id":"c","text":"Send fewer notifications and hope nobody opens the app."},{"id":"d","text":"Turn off the API for the first minute."}]'::jsonb,'b','A notification is a synchronised trigger for your own traffic: a 10 million blast can become a multi-million request spike. Spreading sends over a window flattens the peak, and pre-warmed caches plus load testing make the remaining burst survivable.','Your notification system is a load generator pointed at your own backend.',array['system-design:load-shaping']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["campaign-scheduler","push-pipeline","users","app-api","cache"],"links":[["campaign-scheduler","push-pipeline"],["push-pipeline","users"],["users","app-api"],["app-api","cache"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:odds-board','system-design','odds-board','The Odds Board','It is the IPL final. Your live score page shows the score 30 seconds late, and fans in the group chat are posting spoilers before your page updates.','Live cricket score page for a fantasy app with up to 500,000 simultaneous viewers.','intermediate',array['system-design:polling-vs-push','system-design:server-sent-events','system-design:pub-sub','system-design:fan-out','system-design:hot-keys','system-design:reconnect-storms']::text[],12,'live','Replace polling with push, use SSE for one-way updates, publish once and fan out at the gateway tier, serve the hot score from cache, and make reconnects polite with backoff and jitter. Polling cost is viewers times frequency, push cost is changes. SSE for one-way streams. Pub/sub into a horizontally scaled gateway fleet. 1-second TTL cache for the hot score key. Exponential backoff with jitter on reconnect.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:odds-board:1','system-design:odds-board',1,'initial','design_choice','Today every browser polls /score every 30 seconds. Fans want updates within about a second. Someone suggests polling every second. With 500,000 viewers, what is the problem, and what is the better approach?','Score updates are rare events, but polling asks constantly.','[{"id":"a","text":"Polling every second creates roughly 500,000 requests per second. Switch to server push, where the server sends a message only when the score changes."},{"id":"b","text":"Polling every second is fine. The server can handle it."},{"id":"c","text":"Poll every 5 minutes to save load."},{"id":"d","text":"Ask users to refresh manually."}]'::jsonb,'a','500,000 viewers polling once per second means about 500,000 requests per second, nearly all of which return ''nothing changed''. Push inverts the cost: one message per actual change per connected viewer. Longer polling intervals just make the score staler.','Polling cost scales with viewers times frequency, not with how often anything actually changes.',array['system-design:polling-vs-push']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["viewers","web","score-store"],"links":[["viewers","web"],["web","score-store"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:odds-board:2','system-design:odds-board',2,'probe','design_choice','You choose push. The browser only needs to receive updates (the score, the over, the wicket). It never sends anything back on this channel. WebSocket or Server-Sent Events (SSE)?','Push it is. Which transport?','[{"id":"a","text":"SSE: one-way server-to-client over plain HTTP, with built-in reconnection, which matches the need."},{"id":"b","text":"WebSocket is the only way to push, so use it."},{"id":"c","text":"Polling with a very short interval."},{"id":"d","text":"Email each update."}]'::jsonb,'a','SSE is purpose-built for one-way streams, works through standard HTTP infrastructure, and reconnects automatically. WebSockets are the right call for two-way, low-latency interaction such as chat or games, but add complexity you do not need here. WebSocket is not the only push mechanism.','Choose the simplest transport that fits the data direction.',array['system-design:server-sent-events']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["viewers","push-gateway","score-store"],"links":[["viewers","push-gateway"],["push-gateway","score-store"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:odds-board:3','system-design:odds-board',3,'probe','design_choice','A single machine can hold maybe 50,000 open connections, but you have 500,000 viewers. When the score changes, how do you reach all of them?','One gateway cannot hold everyone. Messages must reach every gateway.','[{"id":"a","text":"Run a fleet of gateway nodes, each holding a share of connections and subscribed to a pub/sub channel for the match. One score update is published once and each gateway forwards it to its own connections."},{"id":"b","text":"Have the score updater open a connection to each of the 500,000 viewers."},{"id":"c","text":"Use one huge machine and hope."},{"id":"d","text":"Store the score in a cookie."}]'::jsonb,'a','Publishing once to a pub/sub backbone and letting each gateway fan the message out to its local connections decouples the producer from the number of viewers. The updater stays simple and the gateway tier scales horizontally. Connecting the updater to every viewer would make it the bottleneck.','Fan-out belongs at the edge of the system, not at the source.',array['system-design:pub-sub','system-design:fan-out']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["score-updater","pub-sub","gateway x10","viewers"],"links":[["score-updater","pub-sub"],["pub-sub","gateway x10"],["gateway x10","viewers"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:odds-board:4','system-design:odds-board',4,'probe','design_choice','Everyone who opens the page also needs the current score immediately, and a million people are doing it around the same wicket. The score of one match is the single hottest key in your system. How do you serve it?','One key, read by everyone.','[{"id":"a","text":"Serve the latest score from an in-memory cache and CDN with a very short TTL (about a second) so the database is read once per interval instead of once per viewer."},{"id":"b","text":"Read the database for every viewer."},{"id":"c","text":"Cache it for an hour."},{"id":"d","text":"Remove the score from the page on load."}]'::jsonb,'a','A hot key read by millions is the ideal case for caching with a tiny TTL: the staleness is below what a viewer can notice, and the origin sees one read per TTL window. An hour-long cache would show wrong scores, and a database read per viewer reproduces the original problem.','Hot keys should be absorbed by caches, with a TTL matched to how fresh the data must be.',array['system-design:hot-keys']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["viewers","cdn","in-memory-cache","score-store"],"links":[["viewers","cdn"],["cdn","in-memory-cache"],["in-memory-cache","score-store"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:odds-board:5','system-design:odds-board',5,'probe','design_choice','During the final over, one gateway restarts and its 50,000 clients all reconnect within the same second, and the retry loops in their apps hammer the system. How do you prevent this reconnect storm from becoming an outage?','Push connections are stateful. When they drop, they all come back at once.','[{"id":"a","text":"Have clients reconnect with exponential backoff plus random jitter, and let the server shed excess connection attempts gracefully."},{"id":"b","text":"Make clients retry every 10 milliseconds until connected."},{"id":"c","text":"Never restart a gateway."},{"id":"d","text":"Ask users to wait."}]'::jsonb,'a','Backoff spaces out retries, and jitter breaks the synchronisation so that 50,000 clients do not all retry at the same instants. Tight retry loops multiply load on a system that is already struggling. Never restarting is not realistic: deploys and failures happen.','Synchronised retries turn a blip into an outage. Randomise client behaviour.',array['system-design:reconnect-storms']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["viewers","load-balancer","gateway x10","cache"],"links":[["viewers","load-balancer"],["load-balancer","gateway x10"],["gateway x10","cache"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:otp-at-midnight','system-design','otp-at-midnight','OTP at Midnight','Your midnight sale starts and OTPs are arriving 40 seconds late, if at all. Meanwhile your SMS bill is exploding and security has noticed someone guessing codes.','E-commerce login via SMS one-time passwords, a single SMS provider, and a flash sale.','intermediate',array['system-design:provider-failover','system-design:async-sending','system-design:otp-lifecycle','system-design:brute-force-protection','system-design:idempotent-sends','system-design:abuse-detection']::text[],12,'live','Make OTP delivery asynchronous with provider failover, keep exactly one short-lived valid code, limit guesses, dedupe sends, and defend the send endpoint against fraud. Queue the send, short timeouts, provider failover. Cooldown, hourly cap, one valid code, 5-minute expiry. 5 attempts per code, throttle by number and IP. Unique send id and send log against duplicates. Country allowlist, bot checks, spend alerts against SMS pumping.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:otp-at-midnight:1','system-design:otp-at-midnight',1,'initial','design_choice','Your login API calls the SMS provider synchronously and waits up to 30 seconds for a response. Under load, the provider slows down and your login API threads all get stuck. What is a robust design?','Everything depends on one slow third party.','[{"id":"a","text":"Raise the timeout to 120 seconds."},{"id":"b","text":"Enqueue the OTP send and return immediately, let workers call providers with short timeouts, and fail over to a second provider when the first is slow or erroring."},{"id":"c","text":"Remove OTP login."},{"id":"d","text":"Retry the same provider in a tight loop."}]'::jsonb,'b','Decoupling with a queue protects the login API from third-party latency, and a short timeout with failover to another provider keeps OTPs flowing when one provider degrades. Longer timeouts and tight retry loops just tie up more of your own capacity.','Never let a third party''s latency become your thread pool''s problem.',array['system-design:provider-failover','system-design:async-sending']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["users","login-api","sms-provider"],"links":[["users","login-api"],["login-api","sms-provider"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:otp-at-midnight:2','system-design:otp-at-midnight',2,'probe','design_choice','Impatient users press ''Resend'' five times and each press generates a new OTP, all of them valid for 10 minutes. This costs money and widens the guessing surface. What should the OTP lifecycle look like?','Delays make people mash the button.','[{"id":"a","text":"Allow unlimited resends and keep all codes valid."},{"id":"b","text":"Enforce a cooldown between resends, cap sends per number per hour, invalidate the previous code when a new one is issued, and use a short expiry (for example 5 minutes)."},{"id":"c","text":"Block the user permanently after one resend."},{"id":"d","text":"Make OTPs valid for 24 hours so nobody needs to resend."}]'::jsonb,'b','Only one code should be valid at a time, so resending invalidates the earlier one. A cooldown and an hourly cap bound cost and abuse, and a short expiry reduces the time window for guessing. Permanent blocks hurt legitimate users and long validity increases risk.','Limit how many valid secrets exist at once, and for how long.',array['system-design:otp-lifecycle']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["users","otp-service","otp-store"],"links":[["users","otp-service"],["otp-service","otp-store"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:otp-at-midnight:3','system-design:otp-at-midnight',3,'probe','design_choice','A 6-digit OTP has 1,000,000 possible values. An attacker scripts guesses against a victim''s number. What protection actually works?','Short numeric codes are guessable by brute force if unlimited.','[{"id":"a","text":"Allow unlimited attempts, because the code is random."},{"id":"b","text":"Limit verification attempts per code (for example 5), invalidate the code after that, and throttle by number and by IP."},{"id":"c","text":"Make the code 4 digits, so it is easier to type."},{"id":"d","text":"Show a hint when the guess is close."}]'::jsonb,'b','With only 5 attempts against 1,000,000 possibilities, an attacker has roughly a 0.0005% chance of success per code, and invalidating the code after the limit prevents continued guessing. Unlimited attempts make brute force trivial, and fewer digits make it worse.','A small secret is safe only when guesses are scarce.',array['system-design:brute-force-protection']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["attacker","verify-api","attempt-counter","otp-store"],"links":[["attacker","verify-api"],["verify-api","attempt-counter"],["verify-api","otp-store"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:otp-at-midnight:4','system-design:otp-at-midnight',4,'probe','design_choice','A worker times out calling provider A. It may have delivered the SMS, so immediately trying provider B could send a second code. What is the safest response?','Failover plus retries can duplicate messages.','[{"id":"a","text":"Record the attempt as unknown, query or await provider A when possible, and delay failover until its outcome is known or the product explicitly accepts a possible duplicate."},{"id":"b","text":"Retry through provider B immediately and assume provider A failed."},{"id":"c","text":"Send through A and B on every request."},{"id":"d","text":"Never retry any timed-out send."}]'::jsonb,'a','A timeout does not tell you whether provider A sent the SMS. A local log and a stable request id help reconcile that uncertainty; a dedupe key only works within a provider that supports it, not automatically across two vendors. Immediate failover may duplicate delivery, while never retrying can strand a user.','Cross-provider failover cannot guarantee one SMS after an unknown outcome.',array['system-design:idempotent-sends']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["otp-queue","sender-workers","provider-A","provider-B","send-log"],"links":[["otp-queue","sender-workers"],["sender-workers","provider-A"],["sender-workers","provider-B"],["sender-workers","send-log"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:otp-at-midnight:5','system-design:otp-at-midnight',5,'probe','design_choice','Your SMS spend triples overnight. Logs show thousands of OTP requests to numbers in countries you do not operate in, from many different IP addresses. What is happening and what do you do?','This is a classic fraud pattern against SMS endpoints.','[{"id":"a","text":"It is SMS pumping fraud. Restrict sending to countries you serve, add per-number, per-device and per-IP limits, add a bot check before sending, and alert on spend anomalies."},{"id":"b","text":"It is normal growth. Increase the SMS budget."},{"id":"c","text":"Turn off SMS for everyone permanently."},{"id":"d","text":"Ask the SMS provider to waive the costs."}]'::jsonb,'a','Attackers trigger OTP sends to premium or colluding numbers to earn a share of the termination fees. Country allowlists, layered rate limits, bot checks on the send endpoint and spend-anomaly alerts cut it off. Raising the budget funds the attack, and disabling SMS for all users punishes everyone.','Anything that sends a message costs you money, so protect the send endpoint like a payment endpoint.',array['system-design:abuse-detection']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["bots","risk-engine","otp-service","sms-providers"],"links":[["bots","risk-engine"],["risk-engine","otp-service"],["otp-service","sms-providers"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:password-change-sessions','system-design','password-change-sessions','A password change that leaves an old phone active','You change your password, but your other phone can still like, comment and send messages. How should the service log it out?','Consumer app with three phone sessions and stateless API workers','foundations',array['system-design:session-revocation','system-design:token-validation']::text[],7,'live','Issue a separate opaque session or token identifier per device. On password change, revoke the other session IDs in an authoritative store. Check revocation on protected requests; a short cache TTL trades read load for a bounded stale window.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:password-change-sessions:1','system-design:password-change-sessions',1,'initial','design_choice','Where should the server keep revocable session identity?',null,'[{"id":"a","text":"A distinct session ID per login in a server-side store"},{"id":"b","text":"Only an expiry timestamp inside each signed token"},{"id":"c","text":"The latest password hash in browser storage"}]'::jsonb,'a','A allows selective revocation. B remains valid until expiry even after a password change. C is client-controlled and cannot revoke another phone.','A signed token proves issuance, not that the session is still allowed.',array['system-design:session-revocation']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:password-change-sessions:2','system-design:password-change-sessions',2,'probe','tradeoff','The user has three phones and wants only the stolen one logged out. What changes?','One phone is reported stolen; the other two are trusted.','[{"id":"a","text":"Revoke that phone’s session ID while retaining the other two"},{"id":"b","text":"Rotate one global signing key for every user"},{"id":"c","text":"Clear the stolen phone’s local storage remotely"}]'::jsonb,'a','A targets one session. B invalidates every user and is operationally costly. C cannot reliably reach an offline or hostile device.','Revocation granularity must match the product action.',array['system-design:session-revocation']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:password-change-sessions:3','system-design:password-change-sessions',3,'probe','design_choice','At 1000 authenticated requests per second, how should workers check revocation?','Each request now needs a revocation decision.','[{"id":"a","text":"Check a short-lived shared cache backed by the authoritative session store"},{"id":"b","text":"Trust the token until its hour-long expiry"},{"id":"c","text":"Read the password table with a full scan on every request"}]'::jsonb,'a','A reduces store reads and bounds staleness by TTL. B leaves the stolen phone active for up to an hour. C makes the hot path unnecessarily expensive.','Cache expiry is part of the security promise.',array['system-design:token-validation']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:password-change-sessions:4','system-design:password-change-sessions',4,'probe','tradeoff','A cache entry lives for 30 seconds. What is the worst ordinary stale window after revocation?','The team proposes a 30-second cache TTL.','[{"id":"a","text":"About 30 seconds unless entries are actively invalidated"},{"id":"b","text":"Zero seconds because the database row is gone"},{"id":"c","text":"One hour because signed tokens cannot be checked"}]'::jsonb,'a','A follows directly from the TTL. B ignores stale cache entries. C ignores the revocation lookup; token expiry is a separate bound.','State the stale window explicitly in the design.',array['system-design:token-validation']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:password-change-sessions:5','system-design:password-change-sessions',5,'probe','design_choice','The revoke operation writes asynchronously. When may the UI say “logged out”?','Product wants a confirmation banner after the password change.','[{"id":"a","text":"After the revocation write is durable; invalidate caches if immediate effect is promised"},{"id":"b","text":"As soon as the queue accepts the job"},{"id":"c","text":"Only after the old phone reconnects and acknowledges"}]'::jsonb,'a','A ties the claim to enforcement. B can leave the old phone posting while the queue waits or fails. C gives an attacker veto power over completion.','Do not claim immediate logout before the enforcement path changes.',array['system-design:session-revocation','system-design:token-validation']::text[],null,null,null) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:result-day','system-design','result-day','Result Day','Every semester your university result site dies at 10:00 sharp, when 300,000 students check their marks within a couple of minutes. This time you own the fix.','University results portal, 300,000 students, results published once per semester at a fixed time.','foundations',array['system-design:read-heavy-traffic','system-design:cdn','system-design:static-precomputation','system-design:thundering-herd','system-design:rate-limiting']::text[],10,'draft','Precompute result pages privately, require each student to authenticate before access, and only then serve their own result through a private cache or short-lived authorized response. Stage the release ahead of time and switch it at publication. Use origin shielding for shared assets, rate limits for abuse, and never treat a roll number or date of birth as authorization.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:result-day:1','system-design:result-day',1,'initial','design_choice','Today each lookup runs a query: SELECT marks FROM results WHERE roll_no = ?. At 10:00 the database falls over. The results never change after publication. What is the best fundamental change?','The traffic is a huge spike, but the data is immutable once published.','[{"id":"a","text":"Buy a much bigger database server."},{"id":"b","text":"Precompute each result privately and serve authorized requests through a cache, without querying marks on every view."},{"id":"c","text":"Add a loading spinner so students wait patiently."},{"id":"d","text":"Ask students to check at different times."}]'::jsonb,'b','Immutable results can be computed once and served many times, but each result remains private. A cache must be behind per-student authorization or use a key that cannot be shared across users. A bigger database alone still faces the publication spike.','If data cannot change, you should not compute it at request time.',array['system-design:read-heavy-traffic','system-design:static-precomputation']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","web","db"],"links":[["students","web"],["web","db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:result-day:2','system-design:result-day',2,'probe','design_choice','You want to avoid database lookups, but each student must see only their own result. How do you lay out the static content?','Static is the plan, but results are per student.','[{"id":"a","text":"One giant file with all 300,000 results that every browser downloads and searches locally."},{"id":"b","text":"One private precomputed object per student, returned only after checking the signed-in student identity, with cache keys scoped to that student."},{"id":"c","text":"Generate each page on demand with a script at the CDN."},{"id":"d","text":"Email results to every student."}]'::jsonb,'b','Small per-student objects avoid a live marks query. The object store must remain private, and every result request must authorize the student before any cached response is served. A public URL based on a roll number would expose marks; one giant download would leak every result.','Precomputation changes the serving path, not the need for authorization.',array['system-design:static-precomputation','system-design:cdn']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","cdn","object-storage"],"links":[["students","cdn"],["cdn","object-storage"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:result-day:3','system-design:result-day',3,'probe','design_choice','The private files are ready before 10:00. How do you publish the new result set on time without exposing it early?','Pre-uploading removes the load but creates an early-leak problem.','[{"id":"a","text":"Upload everything at 10:00:00 and hope it finishes quickly."},{"id":"b","text":"Stage in a private release, then atomically switch the active release pointer at 10:00 while keeping student authorization in front of every result."},{"id":"c","text":"Put a password on the CDN and share it at 10:00."},{"id":"d","text":"Publish early and ask students not to look."}]'::jsonb,'b','The heavy upload can happen ahead of time. Switching the active release pointer is cheap, and the authorization gate remains in place before and after publication. Uploading at 10:00 risks partial availability; a shared password is not access control.','Separate ''prepare'' from ''publish''. Publishing should be one cheap atomic switch.',array['system-design:static-precomputation']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","cdn","object-storage(hidden release)","release-pointer"],"links":[["students","cdn"],["cdn","release-pointer"],["release-pointer","object-storage(hidden release)"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:result-day:4','system-design:result-day',4,'probe','design_choice','At 10:00, thousands of students request the same public result-page shell and release manifest. These shared assets miss the CDN cache together. What reduces the origin spike?','The CDN helps only after objects are cached. The first second is the dangerous one.','[{"id":"a","text":"Enable an origin shield / request coalescing layer so many simultaneous misses become one origin fetch, and pre-warm the cache if possible."},{"id":"b","text":"Lower the CDN cache TTL to 1 second."},{"id":"c","text":"Turn off the CDN so there is only one path to debug."},{"id":"d","text":"Retry failed requests immediately from the browser in a tight loop."}]'::jsonb,'a','Origin shielding and request coalescing help when many clients request the same shared asset. They do not collapse requests for different students’ private results into one fetch. Lowering TTL or tight browser retries amplify the burst.','Caches fail hardest on a cold start. Plan the first second, not just the steady state.',array['system-design:thundering-herd','system-design:cdn']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","cdn-edge","origin-shield","object-storage"],"links":[["students","cdn-edge"],["cdn-edge","origin-shield"],["origin-shield","object-storage"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:result-day:5','system-design:result-day',5,'probe','design_choice','Two problems appear: nervous students refresh every second, and a scraper is trying every roll number from 1 to 400000 to download everyone''s marks. What is the best protection?','The site stands up. Now both enthusiastic and hostile clients show up.','[{"id":"a","text":"Authorize every result against the signed-in student, keep objects private, and rate limit suspicious request patterns at the edge."},{"id":"b","text":"Do nothing, because the data is static."},{"id":"c","text":"Block all traffic that does not come from a university network."},{"id":"d","text":"Make every page 50 MB so scraping is slow."}]'::jsonb,'a','Roll numbers and dates of birth are guessable personal attributes, not authorization. A signed-in identity check prevents one student from requesting another’s result, while rate limits reduce refresh storms and enumeration attempts. Private origin storage prevents direct bypass.','Never use a guessable URL or personal attribute as proof of access.',array['system-design:rate-limiting']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["students","scraper","edge-rate-limit","cdn","object-storage"],"links":[["students","edge-rate-limit"],["scraper","edge-rate-limit"],["edge-rate-limit","cdn"],["cdn","object-storage"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:tatkal-1000','system-design','tatkal-1000','Tatkal 10:00','Tatkal booking opens at 10:00 sharp. 2 lakh people go after 800 seats in the same second, and last time the site was dead by 10:00:01.','Railway-style booking site with a burst of demand against a tiny fixed inventory.','intermediate',array['system-design:thundering-herd','system-design:lock-contention','system-design:virtual-waiting-room','system-design:seat-holds-with-ttl','system-design:fairness','system-design:compensating-actions']::text[],12,'live','Protect the scarce resource: control admission with a waiting room, use short expiring seat holds, randomise within the opening window for fairness, and refund automatically when races still happen. Bottleneck first: hot seat rows and the connection pool. Virtual waiting room to meter demand. Seat holds with TTL, extended once payment starts. Randomised admission plus per-account limits. Automatic compensating refund for late payments.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tatkal-1000:1','system-design:tatkal-1000',1,'initial','design_choice','At 10:00:01 requests start timing out. Web server CPU is moderate, but database connections are exhausted and many queries are waiting on locks for the same few hundred seat rows. What is failing first?','2 lakh requests arrive within a second for 800 seats.','[{"id":"a","text":"The CDN, because it cannot serve images fast enough."},{"id":"b","text":"The database: connection pool exhaustion plus lock contention on a small set of hot rows."},{"id":"c","text":"DNS, because the domain name cannot be resolved."},{"id":"d","text":"The users'' phones."}]'::jsonb,'b','Waiting on locks and running out of connections point at the database. With 800 seats and 2 lakh requesters, almost every request is contending for the same hot rows, so work serialises while connections pile up behind it.','Hot rows serialise writes. More concurrent requests just make the line longer.',array['system-design:lock-contention','system-design:thundering-herd']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["users","web","db(seat rows)"],"links":[["users","web"],["web","db(seat rows)"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tatkal-1000:2','system-design:tatkal-1000',2,'probe','design_choice','You triple the number of web servers and the site still falls over. Why, and what is the right kind of fix?','Scaling the stateless tier did not help.','[{"id":"a","text":"More servers means more concurrent requests reaching the same hot rows. Add a virtual waiting room that admits users at a rate the booking path can handle."},{"id":"b","text":"Add ten times more web servers."},{"id":"c","text":"Remove the database and store seats in browser cookies."},{"id":"d","text":"Return an HTTP 200 with an empty body to everyone."}]'::jsonb,'a','The bottleneck is serialised access to a small set of rows, and extra web servers only increase the pressure. A waiting room (queue with a controlled admission rate) shapes demand to match capacity so the critical section is never flooded. Cookies would let users forge their own seats.','When demand cannot be served, shape it before it reaches the scarce resource.',array['system-design:virtual-waiting-room']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["users","waiting-room","web x3","db(seat rows)"],"links":[["users","waiting-room"],["waiting-room","web x3"],["web x3","db(seat rows)"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tatkal-1000:3','system-design:tatkal-1000',3,'probe','design_choice','A user picks seat 12 and goes to pay. You must keep the seat for them during payment, but you cannot lock it forever if they abandon. What is the right mechanism?','Admission is controlled. Now seat selection needs a safe temporary claim.','[{"id":"a","text":"Mark the seat ''sold'' immediately and refund later if needed."},{"id":"b","text":"Place a short hold on the seat (for example 5 to 10 minutes) with an expiry time; if payment does not complete, the hold expires and the seat becomes available again."},{"id":"c","text":"Hold the seat until the user explicitly cancels."},{"id":"d","text":"Do not hold anything, and let the first payment win."}]'::jsonb,'b','A hold with a TTL gives the user a fair window to pay and guarantees the seat comes back if they disappear. Holds that need an explicit cancel leak seats whenever users close the tab. Marking seats sold early causes mass refunds, and no hold at all means users pay for seats they then cannot get.','Every temporary claim needs an expiry, or abandoned claims silently eat your inventory.',array['system-design:seat-holds-with-ttl']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["waiting-room","booking-service","seat-hold-store(TTL)","payment"],"links":[["waiting-room","booking-service"],["booking-service","seat-hold-store(TTL)"],["booking-service","payment"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tatkal-1000:4','system-design:tatkal-1000',4,'probe','tradeoff','Bots with fast connections are winning the first slots in your waiting room. If you admit strictly by arrival time (FIFO), who benefits, and what is a fairer design?','FIFO sounds fair, but arrival time within one second is mostly network speed.','[{"id":"a","text":"FIFO is perfectly fair. Do nothing."},{"id":"b","text":"Give everyone who arrives in the opening window a random position, verify humans and limit one queue entry per account, so speed advantages stop mattering."},{"id":"c","text":"Admit the fastest 800 requests directly."},{"id":"d","text":"Admit users alphabetically by name."}]'::jsonb,'b','In a burst, arrival order differences of milliseconds reflect latency and automation, not effort. Randomising positions among everyone in the opening window, combined with one entry per verified account, removes the advantage of speed and multi-tab botting. Alphabetical order is arbitrary and unfair in a different way.','Fairness is a design decision. Strict FIFO rewards the fastest machine, not the person.',array['system-design:fairness','system-design:virtual-waiting-room']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["users","bot-check","waiting-room(random within window)","booking-service"],"links":[["users","bot-check"],["bot-check","waiting-room(random within window)"],["waiting-room(random within window)","booking-service"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tatkal-1000:5','system-design:tatkal-1000',5,'probe','design_choice','A user''s payment succeeds at minute 6, but their 5-minute hold expired and another user already bought the seat. What should the system do?','Real payments have variable latency. Edge cases appear.','[{"id":"a","text":"Give the seat to both users."},{"id":"b","text":"Extend the hold when payment begins so slow payments are protected, and if a late payment still finds the seat gone, automatically refund and tell the user."},{"id":"c","text":"Keep the money and apologise."},{"id":"d","text":"Cancel the other user''s ticket."}]'::jsonb,'b','Extending the hold once payment is in flight prevents most races, and a compensating refund handles the leftover cases consistently. Taking the seat from the other user breaks their confirmed booking, and keeping money without a ticket is unacceptable.','Assume timing races will occur and design a compensating action for each one.',array['system-design:compensating-actions']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["payment-gateway","booking-service","seat-hold-store(TTL)","refund-flow"],"links":[["payment-gateway","booking-service"],["booking-service","seat-hold-store(TTL)"],["booking-service","refund-flow"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:the-other-phone','system-design','the-other-phone','The Other Phone','A user changes their account password after losing an old phone. Hours later the old phone is still logged in and can still like, comment and send messages. Your job is to log that session out.','Social app with mobile clients, access tokens, and a backend serving 1,000 requests per second.','intermediate',array['system-design:sessions-vs-jwt','system-design:token-revocation','system-design:multi-device-sessions','system-design:per-request-validation-cost','system-design:propagation-delay']::text[],12,'live','Revocation needs server-side state. Enforce on the server, revoke every other session after a security event, keep the check on a fast store, and bound the stale-trust window with invalidation events and short TTLs. Stateless tokens need a session id or token version to be revocable. Server decides, the device is irrelevant. Password change revokes all sessions except the current one. In-memory validity check on every request. Revocation events plus short TTLs bound the window.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:the-other-phone:1','system-design:the-other-phone',1,'initial','design_choice','Your app issues a signed JWT valid for 30 days. The server validates only the signature and expiry, and stores nothing. After a password change, how can the server reject a token it issued earlier?','Pure stateless tokens cannot be taken back.','[{"id":"a","text":"It cannot, because signed tokens are valid until they expire."},{"id":"b","text":"Add server-side state: track a session id or per-user token version, and reject tokens that do not match the current state."},{"id":"c","text":"Email the old phone and ask it to log out."},{"id":"d","text":"Change the signing key for the whole app every day."}]'::jsonb,'b','A purely stateless token has no way to be revoked. To revoke, the server needs some state to compare against: a session record, a per-user token version number, or a revocation list. Changing the global signing key logs out every user, not just this one.','Revocation requires state. Stateless tokens trade revocability for scale.',array['system-design:sessions-vs-jwt','system-design:token-revocation']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["old-phone","api","auth-service"],"links":[["old-phone","api"],["api","auth-service"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:the-other-phone:2','system-design:the-other-phone',2,'probe','design_choice','A teammate says ''just delete the token from the database''. But the old phone is offline or never calls you again, and the token lives only on that device. Is the old session still dangerous?','You cannot reach into a lost phone.','[{"id":"a","text":"Yes, always, until the token expires on its own."},{"id":"b","text":"No. What matters is the server''s decision: once the session is invalidated server-side, any later request from that phone is rejected, whether or not the device is told."},{"id":"c","text":"Only if you wipe the phone remotely."},{"id":"d","text":"Only if it is connected to Wi-Fi."}]'::jsonb,'b','A token is only useful when presented to your server, and the server decides whether to honour it. Invalidating the session server-side makes the copy on the device worthless the moment it is used, even if the device never hears about it. Remote wipe is unreliable and unnecessary for this.','Enforce on the server, which you control, not on the device, which you do not.',array['system-design:token-revocation']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["old-phone(offline)","api","session-store"],"links":[["old-phone(offline)","api"],["api","session-store"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:the-other-phone:3','system-design:the-other-phone',3,'probe','design_choice','The user is logged in on three phones. They change their password on the phone in their hand. Which sessions should end?','Multiple devices complicate the rule.','[{"id":"a","text":"Only the phone that did not call recently."},{"id":"b","text":"Revoke all other sessions and keep the one performing the change, ideally with a clear message and an option to also sign out everywhere."},{"id":"c","text":"Revoke nothing, because it is annoying for the user."},{"id":"d","text":"Revoke only the oldest session."}]'::jsonb,'b','A password change usually means the user suspects compromise, so every other session should be ended. The device that just authenticated the change can stay signed in. Choosing by last activity or age is arbitrary and leaves risky sessions alive.','Security events should invalidate everything except the proven-fresh session.',array['system-design:multi-device-sessions']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["phone-1(current)","phone-2","phone-3","session-store"],"links":[["phone-1(current)","session-store"],["phone-2","session-store"],["phone-3","session-store"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:the-other-phone:4','system-design:the-other-phone',4,'probe','tradeoff','Checking session validity on every request adds a lookup to a service that was happily handling 1,000 requests per second with no lookups. How do you keep that check cheap?','Revocation now costs a lookup on the hot path.','[{"id":"a","text":"Query the main relational database for every request."},{"id":"b","text":"Check a fast in-memory store or short-lived cache keyed by session id or user token version, with the database as the source of truth behind it."},{"id":"c","text":"Skip the check, since it is too expensive."},{"id":"d","text":"Check validity only at login."}]'::jsonb,'b','A short-lived cache or fast session store can reduce database load, but a remote lookup is not guaranteed to be sub-millisecond. Measure the added latency and choose the cache TTL against the revocation window; sensitive actions may require an authoritative check. Skipping validation defeats revocation.','Put the validity check on a fast store so the hot path stays hot.',array['system-design:per-request-validation-cost']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["clients","api","session-cache","session-db"],"links":[["clients","api"],["api","session-cache"],["session-cache","session-db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:the-other-phone:5','system-design:the-other-phone',5,'probe','design_choice','Session deletion is asynchronous and caches are used. How long can the old phone still post, and how do you shrink that window?','Any cache or async step creates a window of stale trust.','[{"id":"a","text":"Zero seconds. Asynchronous never causes delays."},{"id":"b","text":"Up to the propagation delay plus cache TTL. Shrink it by publishing a revocation event that invalidates caches immediately, keeping TTLs short, and requiring a fresh check for sensitive actions."},{"id":"c","text":"Forever, because nothing can be done."},{"id":"d","text":"Exactly 30 days."}]'::jsonb,'b','Any asynchronous deletion or caching leaves a window where an old session may still be accepted, bounded by event propagation delay and cache TTL. Pushing invalidation events and keeping TTLs short narrows that window, and sensitive operations can bypass the cache for an authoritative check.','Know your staleness bound, and tighten it where the risk is highest.',array['system-design:propagation-delay']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["auth-service","event-bus","api-cache x N","old-phone"],"links":[["auth-service","event-bus"],["event-bus","api-cache x N"],["old-phone","api-cache x N"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:tiffin-run','system-design','tiffin-run','Tiffin Run','You started a tiffin delivery startup in one city. Dispatch is a WhatsApp group. At 12:30 you get 40 orders a minute and 15 delivery partners, and your notes app is on fire.','Single-city tiffin delivery startup moving from WhatsApp dispatch to an app.','foundations',array['system-design:message-queues','system-design:at-least-once-delivery','system-design:idempotent-consumers','system-design:geospatial-index','system-design:state-machine','system-design:timeouts-and-retries']::text[],10,'live','Decouple order acceptance from dispatch with a queue, make consumers idempotent, narrow the partner search with a spatial index, protect status with a state machine, and bound every wait with timeouts. Accept fast, dispatch asynchronously. Idempotent assignment keyed by order id. Geohash or grid cells, plus neighbouring cells. State machine with sequence numbers for status. Offer timeouts, next-candidate fallback, capped retries.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tiffin-run:1','system-design:tiffin-run',1,'initial','design_choice','In your first version, the ''place order'' API finds a delivery partner, waits for them to accept, and only then responds to the customer. At lunch time the app hangs and customers tap Order again and again. How should the flow change?','The order API is slow because it waits on a human to respond.','[{"id":"a","text":"Save the order immediately, respond to the customer, and hand dispatch to a queue processed by background workers."},{"id":"b","text":"Increase the API timeout to 5 minutes."},{"id":"c","text":"Make the customer choose the delivery partner."},{"id":"d","text":"Run dispatch inside the customer''s phone."}]'::jsonb,'a','Accepting the order is fast and certain, whereas finding a partner is slow and uncertain. Splitting them with a queue means customers get an instant confirmation and dispatch can take as long as it needs, with retries. A longer timeout just ties up more server resources while the user stares at a spinner.','Do not make a fast user-facing request wait on a slow, uncertain background process.',array['system-design:message-queues']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["customer-app","order-api","db"],"links":[["customer-app","order-api"],["order-api","db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tiffin-run:2','system-design:tiffin-run',2,'probe','design_choice','Your queue delivers each message at least once. Occasionally a worker crashes after assigning a partner but before acknowledging the message, so the order is dispatched again and two partners show up with the same tiffin. How do you prevent double assignment?','The queue is in place, but ''at least once'' means duplicates happen.','[{"id":"a","text":"Hope workers never crash."},{"id":"b","text":"Make assignment idempotent: record the assignment keyed by order_id with a uniqueness constraint, so a repeated message becomes a no-op."},{"id":"c","text":"Delete the message from the queue before processing it."},{"id":"d","text":"Run only one worker at a time."}]'::jsonb,'b','With at-least-once delivery, duplicates are normal, so the consumer must tolerate them. A unique constraint on the order''s assignment makes the second attempt fail harmlessly. Deleting the message first turns the problem into ''at most once'', which loses orders if the worker crashes. One worker limits throughput and still does not remove the crash window.','Design consumers to be safe under redelivery instead of trying to prevent redelivery.',array['system-design:at-least-once-delivery','system-design:idempotent-consumers']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["order-api","queue","dispatch-workers","db"],"links":[["order-api","queue"],["queue","dispatch-workers"],["dispatch-workers","db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tiffin-run:3','system-design:tiffin-run',3,'probe','algo_choice','The city grows to 5,000 active partners. For each order you scan all partners and compute the distance to find the closest free one. Dispatch is getting slow. What is a better approach?','Dispatch correctness is fixed. Now the nearest-partner search is the slow part.','[{"id":"a","text":"Keep scanning, but buy a faster server."},{"id":"b","text":"Bucket partners into geographic cells (geohash or grid), look up the order''s cell and its neighbours, then rank only those candidates by distance."},{"id":"c","text":"Pick a random partner."},{"id":"d","text":"Sort all partners alphabetically."}]'::jsonb,'b','A spatial index limits the search to partners near the customer instead of everyone in the city. You must check neighbouring cells too, because the nearest partner can be just across a cell boundary. Scanning all partners grows linearly with city size per order, which multiplies badly at lunch peak.','Turn a global scan into a local lookup with a spatial index.',array['system-design:geospatial-index']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["dispatch-workers","geo-index","partner-locations"],"links":[["dispatch-workers","geo-index"],["geo-index","partner-locations"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tiffin-run:4','system-design:tiffin-run',4,'probe','design_choice','Status updates from partner phones arrive out of order on bad networks: the customer sometimes sees DELIVERED and then PICKED_UP. How do you stop that?','Messages from flaky mobile networks do not arrive in the order they were sent.','[{"id":"a","text":"Model order status as a state machine with allowed transitions and a sequence number, and ignore updates that are stale or illegal."},{"id":"b","text":"Always trust the most recently received message."},{"id":"c","text":"Ask partners to retry until the order is right."},{"id":"d","text":"Show customers only the first status."}]'::jsonb,'a','Arrival order is not event order on mobile networks. A defined state machine says DELIVERED cannot be followed by PICKED_UP, and a per-order sequence number or timestamp lets you drop old updates. ''Latest received wins'' is exactly the bug you are seeing.','Never assume messages arrive in the order they were sent.',array['system-design:state-machine']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["partner-app","order-api","order-state-machine","db"],"links":[["partner-app","order-api"],["order-api","order-state-machine"],["order-state-machine","db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:tiffin-run:5','system-design:tiffin-run',5,'probe','design_choice','You offer an order to the nearest partner, but they are busy and never respond, so the order sits unassigned. What should happen?','Humans are unreliable consumers. The system has to keep moving anyway.','[{"id":"a","text":"Wait for the partner as long as it takes."},{"id":"b","text":"Give each offer a short timeout (for example 30 seconds); on expiry or rejection, offer to the next candidate, with a limit on attempts and an alert if none accept."},{"id":"c","text":"Offer the order to all partners at the same time and let them fight."},{"id":"d","text":"Cancel the order after the first non-response."}]'::jsonb,'b','Timeouts turn an unbounded wait into a bounded step, and moving to the next candidate keeps the order flowing. A cap on attempts avoids endless loops and lets you escalate to a human. Broadcasting to everyone invites races and multiple acceptances, and cancelling after one silent partner wastes a sale.','Every external wait needs a timeout, a fallback and an upper bound on retries.',array['system-design:timeouts-and-retries']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["dispatch-workers","offer-timer","partner-app","queue"],"links":[["dispatch-workers","partner-app"],["dispatch-workers","offer-timer"],["offer-timer","queue"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:upi-said-pending','system-design','upi-said-pending','UPI Said Pending','A customer''s bank shows the money debited, but your app says the order failed. They screenshot it, post it, and ten more people report the same thing.','Checkout flow integrated with a payment gateway and UPI, with an order service and an inventory service.','advanced',array['system-design:unknown-outcomes','system-design:idempotency-keys','system-design:reconciliation','system-design:outbox-pattern','system-design:saga-compensation']::text[],14,'live','Treat timeouts as unknown, give every payment a stable identity, verify state through webhooks plus polling plus reconciliation, publish events through an outbox, and compensate with refunds when later steps fail. PENDING is a first-class state. Idempotency key per payment attempt. Webhook, status poller and settlement reconciliation. Transactional outbox for reliable events. Saga with idempotent compensating refund.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:upi-said-pending:1','system-design:upi-said-pending',1,'initial','design_choice','Your code calls the payment gateway. The call times out after 10 seconds, so you mark the order FAILED. Later the gateway confirms the payment succeeded. What was the design mistake?','A timeout is not a failure. It is silence.','[{"id":"a","text":"The timeout was too short, so use 5 minutes."},{"id":"b","text":"Treating a timeout as failure. The outcome is unknown, so the order should be PENDING until the payment state is confirmed."},{"id":"c","text":"Using UPI at all."},{"id":"d","text":"Not retrying fast enough."}]'::jsonb,'b','When a call times out you do not know whether the other side processed it. Modelling an explicit PENDING state avoids declaring failure on money that may already have moved. A longer timeout reduces but never eliminates uncertain outcomes.','Distributed calls have three outcomes: success, failure and unknown. Model all three.',array['system-design:unknown-outcomes']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["checkout","payment-gateway","bank"],"links":[["checkout","payment-gateway"],["payment-gateway","bank"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:upi-said-pending:2','system-design:upi-said-pending',2,'probe','design_choice','While the order is PENDING the user taps Pay again. Without protection, what can happen and how do you prevent it?','Humans retry when screens spin.','[{"id":"a","text":"Nothing bad can happen."},{"id":"b","text":"A second charge may be created. Use a unique payment attempt id per order as an idempotency key, enforce uniqueness in your database, and send it to the gateway so repeats return the original attempt."},{"id":"c","text":"Disable the Pay button forever."},{"id":"d","text":"Show an error and ask the user to call support."}]'::jsonb,'b','Idempotency keys make repeated submissions of the same logical payment collapse into one. Enforcing uniqueness in your own database as well as at the gateway protects against both client double-taps and server retries.','Every money-moving call needs a stable identity.',array['system-design:idempotency-keys']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["user","checkout","payment-gateway","payment-attempts-db"],"links":[["user","checkout"],["checkout","payment-gateway"],["checkout","payment-attempts-db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:upi-said-pending:3','system-design:upi-said-pending',3,'probe','design_choice','PENDING orders must eventually become SUCCESS or FAILED. The gateway sends webhooks, but webhooks can be delayed, duplicated or lost. How do you resolve pending payments reliably?','Waiting for a webhook alone is not reliable.','[{"id":"a","text":"Rely only on webhooks."},{"id":"b","text":"Combine idempotent webhook handling with a scheduled job that polls the gateway''s status API for old PENDING orders, plus a daily reconciliation against the gateway''s settlement report."},{"id":"c","text":"Mark all PENDING orders as failed after 1 minute."},{"id":"d","text":"Ask users to confirm their payment by email."}]'::jsonb,'b','Webhooks are an optimisation, not a guarantee. A poller catches the lost ones, and reconciliation against settlement reports catches anything else, giving you a final source of truth. Failing every pending order after a minute recreates the original bug.','Never trust a single notification channel. Verify state by asking.',array['system-design:reconciliation']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["gateway","webhook-handler","status-poller","order-db","settlement-file"],"links":[["gateway","webhook-handler"],["status-poller","gateway"],["webhook-handler","order-db"],["status-poller","order-db"],["settlement-file","order-db"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:upi-said-pending:4','system-design:upi-said-pending',4,'probe','design_choice','Your service marks the order PAID in the database and then publishes an ''OrderPaid'' event so shipping can start. The service crashes between the two steps. The order is paid, but shipping never hears about it. What pattern fixes this?','Two separate writes cannot be made atomic without help.','[{"id":"a","text":"Publish the event first, then update the database."},{"id":"b","text":"Transactional outbox: write the order update and the event to an outbox table in the same database transaction, and have a relay publish outbox rows reliably, with consumers handling duplicates."},{"id":"c","text":"Hope the service does not crash."},{"id":"d","text":"Skip the event and make shipping poll every order."}]'::jsonb,'b','Writing the state change and the event record in one database transaction removes the crash window, and the relay guarantees the event is eventually published. Because the relay may publish twice, consumers must be idempotent. Publishing first has the same problem in reverse.','Never rely on two independent writes (database and message bus) staying in sync.',array['system-design:outbox-pattern']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["order-service","order-db","outbox-table","relay","event-bus","shipping"],"links":[["order-service","order-db"],["order-service","outbox-table"],["relay","outbox-table"],["relay","event-bus"],["event-bus","shipping"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:upi-said-pending:5','system-design:upi-said-pending',5,'probe','design_choice','A payment succeeds, but when you try to confirm the order the inventory service says the item just sold out. The money has already been taken. What is the correct handling?','A multi-step business flow that cannot be rolled back with one database transaction.','[{"id":"a","text":"Keep the money and cancel silently."},{"id":"b","text":"Run a compensating step: automatically refund (idempotently) and notify the customer, treating the flow as a saga with an explicit compensation for each step."},{"id":"c","text":"Roll back the bank''s database."},{"id":"d","text":"Wait for the customer to complain."}]'::jsonb,'b','Across services there is no single transaction that can undo everything, so each step needs an explicit compensating action. The refund must itself be idempotent so repeated attempts never refund twice. Staying silent or waiting for complaints damages trust and may breach regulations.','If you cannot roll back, you must design the roll-forward undo.',array['system-design:saga-compensation']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["order-service","payment","inventory","refund-service"],"links":[["order-service","payment"],["order-service","inventory"],["order-service","refund-service"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenarios(id,course_id,slug,title,hook,context,tier,concept_ids,estimated_minutes,status,solution_brief) values('system-design:wheres-my-driver','system-design','wheres-my-driver','Where''s My Driver','Your ride-hailing app has 1 million active drivers, and each one sends a GPS ping every 4 seconds. Riders want to see nearby cars instantly and watch their driver move on the map.','Ride-hailing location pipeline: ingestion, nearby search, and live tracking.','advanced',array['system-design:write-heavy-ingestion','system-design:latest-state-store','system-design:geospatial-index','system-design:cell-boundaries','system-design:push-tracking']::text[],14,'live','Compute the write rate first (250k per second), store only latest positions hot with TTL, index by geospatial cells with neighbour queries, and push targeted updates with client interpolation. 1M drivers divided by 4s is 250,000 writes per second. Latest position in memory with TTL, history on a stream. Geohash, S2 or H3 cells with neighbour lookups. Move cell membership as drivers cross boundaries. Targeted push plus client interpolation for tracking.') on conflict(id) do update set title=excluded.title,hook=excluded.hook,context=excluded.context,tier=excluded.tier,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,estimated_minutes=excluded.estimated_minutes,status=excluded.status,solution_brief=excluded.solution_brief;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:wheres-my-driver:1','system-design:wheres-my-driver',1,'initial','design_choice','1,000,000 drivers send one ping every 4 seconds. Roughly how many location writes per second must you ingest, and what does that suggest about how you store them?','Start with the back-of-the-envelope number.','[{"id":"a","text":"About 250,000 writes per second. Use a write-optimised path, such as an in-memory store for the latest position and a stream or log for history, rather than one durable relational row per ping."},{"id":"b","text":"About 250,000 writes per second. Keep latest positions in a store built for high update rates and stream history separately; capacity-test the chosen storage design."},{"id":"c","text":"About 4 million writes per second, so it is impossible."},{"id":"d","text":"About 1,000 writes per second, so any database works."}]'::jsonb,'a','1,000,000 divided by 4 seconds is 250,000 updates per second. The matching path needs the latest position, while trip history has different retention and durability needs. A partitioned relational design may work at this rate, but it needs evidence from capacity tests; writing every ping as a durable row in an unplanned single table is risky.','Calculate ingest rate first, then separate latest-state reads from historical storage.',array['system-design:write-heavy-ingestion']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["drivers","ingest-api","storage"],"links":[["drivers","ingest-api"],["ingest-api","storage"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:wheres-my-driver:2','system-design:wheres-my-driver',2,'probe','design_choice','Matching a rider to a driver only needs where each driver is now. Do you need to keep every ping forever in the hot path?','Most of those 250,000 writes per second are immediately obsolete.','[{"id":"a","text":"Yes. Every ping must be in the hot store forever."},{"id":"b","text":"No. Keep only the latest position per driver, with a TTL so offline drivers disappear, and send a sampled or batched history to a stream for trips, analytics and disputes."},{"id":"c","text":"Drop all pings and rely on the driver app to report on request."},{"id":"d","text":"Keep only one ping per day."}]'::jsonb,'b','The hot path needs current state, which overwrites itself. A TTL naturally removes drivers who stop reporting, and sampling or batching history protects cost while preserving what you need for trip records. Hot storage of every ping forever is expensive and slows the queries that matter.','Separate current state (small, hot) from history (large, cold).',array['system-design:latest-state-store']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["ingest-api","latest-location-store(TTL)","location-stream","cold-storage"],"links":[["ingest-api","latest-location-store(TTL)"],["ingest-api","location-stream"],["location-stream","cold-storage"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:wheres-my-driver:3','system-design:wheres-my-driver',3,'probe','algo_choice','A rider requests a ride. How do you find drivers within 2 km without comparing the rider to all 1,000,000 drivers?','The naive scan is O(N) per request.','[{"id":"a","text":"Scan all drivers and compute the distance to each."},{"id":"b","text":"Index drivers by a geospatial cell (geohash, S2 or H3), look up the rider''s cell and the neighbouring cells, then rank the small candidate set by distance."},{"id":"c","text":"Sort drivers by their user id."},{"id":"d","text":"Ask drivers who are nearby to raise their hand."}]'::jsonb,'b','Cell-based indexing converts a global search into a local one: the cost depends on the number of drivers in a few cells, not the whole fleet. Scanning a million drivers per request would be too slow and wasteful at ride-request rates.','Index by location so the search cost scales with local density, not total size.',array['system-design:geospatial-index']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["rider-request","geo-index(cells)","latest-location-store(TTL)"],"links":[["rider-request","geo-index(cells)"],["geo-index(cells)","latest-location-store(TTL)"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:wheres-my-driver:4','system-design:wheres-my-driver',4,'probe','design_choice','Riders standing near a cell edge sometimes see no nearby drivers, even though a driver is 100 metres away across the boundary. Also, a driver who drove into a new cell appears in two cells for a while. What are the two bugs?','Cells are boundaries, not neighbourhoods.','[{"id":"a","text":"No bugs. Boundaries are part of geography."},{"id":"b","text":"You must query the neighbouring cells as well as the rider''s cell, and on each location update you must remove the driver from the old cell as you add them to the new one (with expiry as a safety net)."},{"id":"c","text":"Make cells one metre wide."},{"id":"d","text":"Disable the map near cell edges."}]'::jsonb,'b','A nearby driver can sit just across a cell boundary, so the query must cover adjacent cells. Membership also has to move when a driver crosses a boundary, or stale entries linger; a TTL cleans up anything missed. Tiny cells would explode the number of cells to query.','Spatial indexes create edge cases at their borders. Query neighbours and move members.',array['system-design:cell-boundaries']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["rider","cell A","cell B","driver"],"links":[["rider","cell A"],["driver","cell B"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
insert into public.arena_scenario_steps(id,scenario_id,position,kind,step_type,prompt,context_note,options,correct_option_id,explanation,bottleneck_note,concept_ids,code_snippet,language,system_state) values('system-design:wheres-my-driver:5','system-design:wheres-my-driver',5,'probe','design_choice','After a ride is booked, the rider''s app polls for the driver''s position every second, and 200,000 riders are tracking at once. How do you make live tracking efficient?','Tracking is a one-to-one stream that updates frequently.','[{"id":"a","text":"Keep every rider polling every second."},{"id":"b","text":"Push position updates to only the rider tracking that driver through a persistent connection, and interpolate movement on the client, with ping frequency adjusted when the car is stationary."},{"id":"c","text":"Show the driver''s position only once, when booked."},{"id":"d","text":"Broadcast every driver''s position to every rider."}]'::jsonb,'b','Only the booked rider needs a given driver''s updates, so targeted push avoids 200,000 constant polls. Client-side interpolation makes the motion look smooth at lower update rates, and adaptive ping frequency saves bandwidth, battery and backend load. Broadcasting everyone to everyone is wasteful and a privacy problem.','Send each update only to those who need it, and let the client smooth the gaps.',array['system-design:push-tracking']::text[],null,null,'{"nodes":["driver","ingest-api","pub-sub","tracking-gateway","rider-apps"],"links":[["driver","ingest-api"],["ingest-api","pub-sub"],["pub-sub","tracking-gateway"],["tracking-gateway","rider-apps"]]}'::jsonb) on conflict(id) do update set prompt=excluded.prompt,context_note=excluded.context_note,options=excluded.options,correct_option_id=excluded.correct_option_id,explanation=excluded.explanation,bottleneck_note=excluded.bottleneck_note,concept_ids=excluded.concept_ids,code_snippet=excluded.code_snippet,language=excluded.language,system_state=excluded.system_state;
